      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we need to express both logarithms with the same base or convert them into exponential form to solve for \\(x\\).\n\nFirst, we use the change of base formula for logarithms. The change of base formula states that \\(\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}\\). We can use the natural logarithm (base \\(e\\)) for convenience:\n\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}\n\\]\n\nNext, we express \\(\\log_{27} 3\\) in terms of base 3:\n\n\\[\n27 = 3^3 \\implies \\log_{27} 3 = \\log_{3^3} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 3^3} = \\frac{\\log 3}{3 \\log 3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute this back into our original equation:\n\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis means:\n\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3} \\implies x^{1/3} = 4\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(x\\), we raise both sides of the equation to the power of 3:\n\n\\[\n\\left(x^{1/3}\\right)^3 = 4^3 \\implies x = 64\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(x\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]",
