      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let's solve the equation $\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3$. To do this, we need to express both logarithms in terms of base 10 or natural logarithms for easier manipulation.\n\nFirst, recall the change of base formula for logarithms: $\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}$. Using this formula, we can rewrite the given equation in terms of a common base, say base 10.\n\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log_{10} 4}{\\log_{10} x}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log_{10} 3}{\\log_{10} 27}\n\\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log_{10} 4}{\\log_{10} x} = \\frac{\\log_{10} 3}{\\log_{10} 27}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the right-hand side. Since $27 = 3^3$, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10} 27 = \\log_{10} (3^3) = 3 \\log_{10} 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation now is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log_{10} 4}{\\log_{10} x} = \\frac{\\log_{10} 3}{3 \\log_{10} 3}\n\\]\n\nWe can cancel $\\log_{10} 3$ from the denominator and numerator on the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log_{10} 4}{\\log_{10} x} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10} 4 = \\frac{1}{3} \\log_{10} x\n\\]\n\nRewriting the right-hand side using the property of logarithms $\\log_a b^c = c \\log_a b$, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10} 4 = \\log_{10} x^{1/3}\n\\]\n\nSince the bases are the same, we can equate the arguments:\n\n\\[\n4 = x^{1/3}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for $x$, we cube both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\nx = 4^3 = 64\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]",
