      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve for \\(x\\) in the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we need to express both logarithms in terms of base 10 or natural logarithms for easier manipulation. However, a simpler approach is to express both logarithms with the same base, in this case, base 27.\n\nFirst, we use the change of base formula for logarithms, which states that \\(\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}\\). Applying this to \\(\\log_{27} 3\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}\n\\]\nSince \\(27 = 3^3\\), we have \\(\\log 27 = \\log (3^3) = 3 \\log 3\\). Therefore,\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{3 \\log 3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nSo the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\) becomes:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nThis means that \\(x\\) is the number such that \\(x^{1/3} = 4\\). To find \\(x\\), we cube both sides of the equation:\n\\[\nx = 4^3 = 64\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{64}\\).",
